# Accessibility Best Practices

## Semantic HTML

- Use proper HTML elements for their intended purpose
- Use `<button>` for actions, `<a>` for navigation
- Use heading hierarchy properly (h1 → h2 → h3)
- Use `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<article>`, `<aside>` appropriately
- Use `<form>` for data input
- Avoid `<div>` and `<span>` for interactive elements

## ARIA Attributes

- Use ARIA only when semantic HTML is insufficient
- Prefer semantic HTML over ARIA when possible
- Use `aria-label` and `aria-labelledby` for accessible names
- Use `aria-describedby` for additional descriptions
- Implement `aria-live` regions for dynamic content
- Use `aria-hidden` to hide decorative elements from screen readers
- Set `aria-expanded`, `aria-selected`, `aria-checked` for state

## Keyboard Navigation

- Ensure all interactive elements are keyboard accessible
- Support Tab, Enter, Space, Escape, and Arrow keys
- Implement proper focus order (tab index)
- Don't trap keyboard focus unintentionally
- Show visible focus indicators
- Support keyboard shortcuts where appropriate
- Test navigation with keyboard only

## Focus Management

- Set focus appropriately after actions (modal open, page load)
- Return focus after modal/dialog closes
- Skip to main content link for keyboard users
- Show clear focus indicators (outline, ring)
- Don't remove focus styles without replacement
- Handle focus in dynamic content
- Use `autoFocus` prop appropriately

## Forms Accessibility

- Label all form inputs with `<label>` element
- Use `htmlFor` to associate labels with inputs
- Group related inputs with `<fieldset>` and `<legend>`
- Provide clear error messages
- Use `aria-invalid` and `aria-describedby` for errors
- Show required fields clearly
- Provide helpful placeholder text (don't rely on it)
- Support autocomplete attributes

## Images and Media

- Provide alt text for all images
- Use empty alt (`alt=""`) for decorative images
- Provide captions for videos
- Provide transcripts for audio content
- Don't convey information through images alone
- Use proper contrast for text overlays
- Provide audio descriptions when needed

## Color and Contrast

- Maintain WCAG AA contrast ratio (4.5:1 for normal text)
- Don't rely on color alone to convey information
- Test with high contrast mode
- Support dark mode properly
- Ensure focus indicators have sufficient contrast
- Test with color blindness simulators
- Use patterns or labels in addition to color

## Screen Readers

- Test with screen readers (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver)
- Provide meaningful link text (not "click here")
- Use descriptive button labels
- Announce dynamic content changes
- Use `aria-live` for notifications
- Hide decorative icons from screen readers
- Provide context for ambiguous elements

## Dynamic Content

- Use `aria-live` for content updates
- Set `aria-busy` during loading
- Announce errors and success messages
- Manage focus after content changes
- Provide loading states
- Handle content removal gracefully
- Support prefers-reduced-motion

## Interactive Components

- Make custom components keyboard accessible
- Implement proper ARIA roles for custom widgets
- Support expected keyboard interactions
- Provide accessible names and descriptions
- Indicate state changes (expanded, selected, checked)
- Use native elements when possible (select, checkbox)
- Test with assistive technologies

## Navigation

- Provide skip links to main content
- Use proper heading hierarchy
- Implement breadcrumb navigation with ARIA
- Mark current page in navigation
- Provide multiple ways to navigate
- Support landmarks properly
- Include a sitemap for complex sites

## Testing

- Use automated testing (jest-axe, Lighthouse)
- Test with keyboard only
- Test with screen readers
- Test with browser zoom (200%+)
- Test with high contrast mode
- Test with color blindness simulators
- Conduct user testing with people with disabilities

## shadcn/ui Components

- Use accessible shadcn/ui components
- Customize while maintaining accessibility
- Test custom variants for accessibility
- Use proper ARIA attributes with components
- Implement keyboard interactions properly
- Test with assistive technologies
- Follow Radix UI accessibility guidelines

## Best Practices

- Consider accessibility from the start
- Follow WCAG 2.1 Level AA guidelines
- Test regularly with assistive technologies
- Include people with disabilities in user testing
- Provide accessibility documentation
- Train team on accessibility standards
- Monitor accessibility in CI/CD
- Stay updated on accessibility best practices
